# Test fixtures are byte-exact copies of files Brauser wrote; never convert
# their line endings on checkout (Windows CI would otherwise see CRLF).
host/tests/fixtures/** -text

# Bookkeeping files every worker cycle, queue PR and human PR touches merge
# without conflicts once the drivers are registered in a clone
# (`sh scripts/install-merge-drivers.sh`; without them git does a normal text
# merge, and `python3 scripts/render_backlog.py --resolve` finishes it):
# - the beads queue merges bead by bead (newer updated_at wins a field both
#   sides changed);
# - BOSUN_FEATURE_BACKLOG.md merges its hand-written text and re-renders its
#   generated blocks from the merged queue;
# - the append-only logs keep both sides' new entries (git's built-in union
#   driver, which needs no registration).
# None of these drivers runs in GitHub's mergeability check or merge queue,
# which merge as plain text. The improvement feed avoids needing one: each
# entry is its own file under docs/improvement-feed/ (BRAUSER_IMPROVEMENT_FEED.md).
.beads/issues.jsonl merge=beads-jsonl
BOSUN_FEATURE_BACKLOG.md merge=bosun-backlog
PENDING_AMOS_DOC_UPDATES.md merge=union
CookieFeature.md merge=union

# SUMMARY.md is compiled into the host as the summary prompt; its template
# delimiters are LF-only, so never let a checkout convert it to CRLF.
SUMMARY.md text eol=lf

# generate-starters.mjs writes LF and compares raw bytes in --check; keep
# these checked out as LF so Windows CI doesn't see a false "out of date".
extension/starter-families.generated.ts text eol=lf
protocol/starters/*.json text eol=lf
